  "summary": "Distinctive conical minarets characteristic of Khorezm region, featuring cylindrical bases tapering to slender peaks with decorative brickwork patterns.",
  "description": "These minarets were constructed using distinctive baked brick techniques with intricate geometric and epigraphic patterns, reflecting the region's unique architectural evolution separate from broader Persian traditions. They served as both religious landmarks and symbols of the Khorezmian architectural identity.",
  "culturalContext": "Khorezm's conical minarets — Urgench's Kutlug Timur (60m, 12th-14th c.), Uzbekistan's tallest, with Turkmenistan's Köneürgenç siblings — are the Central Asian brick tower's other branch: not Samarkand's cylindrical taper but stepped conical profiles with turquoise glazed bands and muqarnas lantern crowns, built by Khwarazmian masters along the Amu Darya. Earthquake-scarred but standing, they anchor the UNESCO-listed \"monumental bricks\" of the Khwarezm oasis and the Ghaznavid-to-Timurid tower lineage.",
